  "geometric_reasoning": [
    "The first-wave conservative exact T requires five light cells and every orthogonal border cell to be non-light. A heart four is a disjoint four-cell straight template.",
    "For the 5x4 geometry, the minimum union of a T border and a disjoint four template is eight cells. This bound ignores all path, thorn, spinner-location and fixed-cell restrictions, so it is optimistic.",
    "When spinner 19 is light, the board has thirteen light and only seven non-light cells. Seven cannot fill the eight-cell minimum; the requested two goals cannot hold in every spinner phase under the current conservative T policy.",
    "This is a layout-space bound, not a route-search timeout and not a statement about the less conservative native shape boundary. No first-wave policy was relaxed.",
    "A written eight-separator witness provides a boundary regression. The independent geometry oracle and production precheck have not run yet."
  ],
  "changes": [
    "Add a necessary separator-space precheck for single-color T plus a different single-color four. Fail only if even arbitrary layout fails in one shared phase; alternative per-phase layouts are allowed by the bound.",
    "Return the appended IncompatibleGoals status with no route and an explicit space/shape message. Other search misses retain their unknown-feasibility wording.",
    "Keep the source-only evaluation cache and focused spinner search from the previous recorded-board investigation.",
    "User explicitly extended the existing allowPoisonWithHeartFour team policy to mortal poison. The setting remains default-off; enabled settings now accept both poisons only with a first-wave exact straight heart four in every shared phase.",
    "Jammers already clear normally. Bombs may clear but all must be removed in the first wave. Thorn survival and native unmatchable rules remain independently enforced.",
    "Search ranking no longer prioritizes avoiding accepted mortal poison over goals/Combo. Total poison and mortal counts remain independently reported."
  ],
